  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAfter a break reprieve from the well-above normal temperatures, the \nsummerlike heat returns on Tuesday as a near-record breaking \nmidlevel ridge builds back into the mid-Mississippi Valley. Highs in \nthe mid 90s are expected, though heat index are not forecast to be \nmuch above actual air temperatures due to lingering lower humidity \nlevels.\n\nA strong mid/upper level trough is forecast to cross the Great \nLakes/southeastern Canada on Wednesday, with a surface low crossing \nHudson Bay. A cold front draped to its southwest will move through \nour region late Wednesday/Wednesday night. This front has sped up a \nbit compared to how it looked yesterday, which does induce more \ntemperature uncertainty for much of the area. In addition, ongoing \nshowers and thunderstorms are forecast near the boundary with cloud \ncover debris also potentially keeping temperatures a bit lower. We \nare now looking at highs most likely in the low to mid 90s for most \nlocations, though the exact frontal timing is key. A slower (faster) \nfront would likely yield higher (lower) temperatures than forecast. \nDewpoints will also be coming up ahead of the front, so peak heat \nindex values around 105 degrees (magnitude heat advisory criteria) \nare possible in parts of central and east-central Missouri as well \nas southwest Illinois ahead of the boundary. \n\nThe slow-moving cold front will also be the best chance of \nwidespread rainfall in at least 2 weeks, if not longer. PoPs \ncontinue to increase with each successive forecast cycle, with the \nbest chances (70-80%) Wednesday afternoon through early Thursday \nmorning. Probabilities for at least 0.25\" is above 50% areawide, \nwith 0.50\"+ topping out around 50% along the I-70 corridor in \nMissouri. There may also be a low threat for a few strong to severe \nshowers and thunderstorms given a semi-favorable environment (30+ \nknots of deep-layer shear and at least moderate instability) for \norganized convection along the frontal boundary itself Wednesday \nafternoon/evening. \n\nForecast uncertainty heading toward next weekend remains high due to \na lot of spread with where the anomalous mid/upper level ridging \nwill be centered at the center of the omega block. While only one of \nthe four clusters of the 500-hPa height pattern show the ridge \nmoving back into the mid-Mississippi Valley, that cluster is \ncomprised of a plurality (35%) of LREF members. If this occurs, a \nreturn to a hotter and dry pattern could transpire. The other three \nclusters have quasi-zonal flow with ridging to the south or west. \nThis type of pattern should yield at least slightly cooler \ntemperatures (but still likely above normal) as well as additional \nchances of showers and thunderstorms.",
